	"narrative": {
		"northStar": "The Recruiter's Highlighter",
		"overview": "The page is a clean CV on porcelain paper, and the only color on it is the highlighter a recruiter would use to mark what fits the role. Everything a visitor does (pasting a posting, picking a role, asking a question) moves that highlighter: chips light up, matching tiles come to the front and their diagrams turn from ink and gray to lime, the numbers that prove it sit on a lime underline. Above the fold, soft matte clay objects (a circuit-board chip, a floor plan, a shopping bag with a camera, a terminal, a car with a price tag, a dumbbell) float around the name over soft contact shadows; each one is a piece of his work, steps forward when that work matches, and opens it on click.\n\nDensity is low and the type is large: one name set wide and heavy, one plain reading face, generous air. The page is built from three stacked sheets: the porcelain hero where the 3D lives, a white paper sheet for the CV and the catalogue that slides up over it, and an ink sheet that closes the page with the resume and contact. Motion is quick and purposeful: one entrance, a scroll drift in the 3D layer, and a re-ranking animation when the catalogue changes.",
		"keyCharacteristics": [
			"Porcelain, paper and ink neutrals with a single highlighter lime that always means \"this matches\" or \"act here\".",
			"Mona Sans throughout: wide and heavy for display, plain for reading, proportional figures so every zero is plain.",
			"Three layered sheets with large rounded tops (28px) as the depth model.",
			"Tile art drawn in code as geometric diagrams of each project's own mechanism.",
			"3D clay objects in white, light gray, ink and lime, lit from the upper left over soft contact shadows, living only behind the hero."
		],
		"rules": [
			{
				"name": "The Highlighter Rule",
				"body": "Lime marks a match or the next action, and nothing else. If an element is lime, a visitor should be able to say what it matches or what pressing it does. Tile diagrams are ink and gray at rest; their accent layer fades to lime (0.5s) only when the tile matches.",
				"section": "colors"
			},
			{
				"name": "The No Gray Text On Lime Rule",
				"body": "Text on lime is always ink.",
				"section": "colors"
			},
			{
				"name": "The Plain Zero Rule",
				"body": "Never turn on tabular figures. Mona Sans's tabular set uses a slashed zero, and the brief asks for a plain one.",
				"section": "typography"
			},
			{
				"name": "The Highlighted Figure Rule",
				"body": "A number that proves a claim (20M+, 87%, 10x) is set heavier and sits on a lime band covering its lower 44%. It stays inline in its sentence; it never becomes a big-number stat tile. Years are not proof numbers and get no band.",
				"section": "typography"
			},
			{
				"name": "The Three Sheets Rule",
				"body": "Porcelain hero, white paper, ink close. A new layer of the page is a new sheet with a 28px rounded top (22px on phones), never a boxed card floating on the ground.",
				"section": "elevation"
			},
			{
				"name": "The One Elevation Rule",
				"body": "An element gets an outline or a shadow, never both. Chips and inputs use outlines; floating surfaces use shadows.",
				"section": "elevation"
			}
		],
		"dos": [
			"Do keep lime for matches and actions (selected chips, match marks, highlighted figures, the send key, the per-job banner).",
			"Do draw tile art as geometry of the work's own mechanism, in ink and grays, with its accent layer on `var(--art-accent)` so it lights up only on a match.",
			"Do set proof numbers inline with the lime highlighter band, in a full sentence.",
			"Do keep the 3D layer behind the hero only, and swap it for the still image under reduced motion or without WebGL.",
			"Do animate transform and opacity only, with the ease-out curve (cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1))."
		],
		"donts": [
			"Don't turn on tabular figures (the zero becomes slashed).",
			"Don't use lime as a text color on paper or porcelain, or as decoration with no match behind it.",
			"Don't add kickers or eyebrow labels above headings; meta lines go under the title.",
			"Don't use big-number stat tiles; numbers stay inside sentences.",
			"Don't put a photo of him, his phone number or company logos on the page; the only screenshot is the supplied shop.com image for the Shopify tile.",
			"Don't add em dashes to copy."
		]
